## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about DAGs, timing, and dynamic programming over graphs, a common pattern in build pipelines, data workflows, or task scheduling.

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort to linearize a DAG
- DP on DAG to propagate earliest start times
- In-degree based BFS (Kahn) and cycle detection

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestTimes(n, duration, edges) {
  const g = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { g[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const es = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let visited = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    visited++;
    for (const v of g[u]) {
      es[v] = Math.max(es[v], es[u] + duration[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const total = Math.max(...es.map((s, i) => s + duration[i]));
  return { earliestStart: es, totalDuration: total };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to detect cycles and report the cycle path?
- How would you extend to handle parallel resources or varying durations per edge?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply DFS for topological ordering and then DP-style relaxation on a DAG to compute shortest paths without a heap. Also checks path reconstruction via a parent array.

## Key Concepts

- DFS-based topological sort
- DAG single-source shortest path via relaxation in topo order
- Path reconstruction with a parent array
- Complexity: O(n+m)

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(n, edges, start, target) {
  const g = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) g[u].push([v, w]);
  const visited = Array(n).fill(false);
  const topo = [];
  function dfs(u){
    visited[u] = true;
    for (const [v] of g[u]) if (!visited[v]) dfs(v);
    topo.push(u);
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (!visited[i]) dfs(i);
  topo.reverse();
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);
  dist[start] = 0;
  for (const u of topo) {
    if (dist[u] === INF) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of g[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        parent[v] = u;
      }
    }
  }
  if (dist[target] === INF) return {time: -1, path: []};
  const path = [];
  for (let x = target; x != -1; x = parent[x]) path.push(x);
  path.reverse();
  return {time: dist[target], path};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle multiple sources or tie-breaking paths?
- How would you adapt if the graph could contain cycles (cycle detection and fallback to Dijkstra)?

## Why This Is Asked

The problem mirrors real-world build systems and DAG validations, requiring both cycle detection and a valid execution order.

## Key Concepts

- DFS with 3-color marking to detect cycles
- Topological sort via postorder push
- Handling multiple components in a possibly disconnected graph

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfsTopo(graph) {
  const n = graph.length;
  const color = Array(n).fill(0); // 0 white, 1 gray, 2 black
  const order = [];
  const cycles = [];
  function dfs(u) {
    color[u] = 1;
    for (const v of graph[u] || []) {
      if (color[v] === 0) {
        if (dfs(v)) return true;
      } else if (color[v] === 1) {
        cycles.push([u, v]);
        return true;
      }
    }
    color[u] = 2;
    order.push(u);
    return false;
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (color[i] === 0) {
      if (dfs(i)) break;
    }
  }
  return { topo: order.reverse(), cycle: cycles.length ? cycles : null };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you return the exact cycle path rather than just edge pairs?
- What are the time and space complexities for dense vs sparse graphs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a DP with variable-length segments and per-segment value, plus both memoized and tabulated solutions.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over positions with limited jump length
- Maintaining per-iteration maximums within a window
- Bottom-up and top-down (memoization) DP variants

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(arr, k) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    let maxInWin = -Infinity;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k && i + t <= n; t++) {
      maxInWin = Math.max(maxInWin, arr[i + t - 1]);
      dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], maxInWin + dp[i + t]);
    }
  }
  return dp[0];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize the inner max to O(n) using a deque when k is large?
- How would you reconstruct the actual partition that yields the maximum sum?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a stable multi-key sort and apply bit-operations in a practical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Two-key comparator
- Hamming weight (popcount)
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortByWeightAndValue(arr) {
  const popcount = (n) => {
    n = n >>> 0;
    n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
    n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
    n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
    return (n * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
  };
  const items = arr.map(v => ({ val: v, w: popcount(v) }));
  const mergeSort = (a) => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a.slice();
    const m = Math.floor(a.length / 2);
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i].w !== R[j].w) res.push(L[i].w < R[j].w ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
      else res.push(L[i].val <= R[j].val ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
    }
    while (i < L.length) res.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) res.push(R[j++]);
    return res;
  };
  const sorted = mergeSort(items);
  return sorted.map(o => o.val);
}
```
